{:edb/db-before-t 1503 :edb/db-after-t 1504 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534816 50 #inst "2026-10-08T07:59:06.661Z" 13194139534816 true] [17592186155293 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155295 13194139534816 true] [17592186155296 1104 #edb/ref 17592186155298 13194139534816 true] [17592186156982 1100 "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x$, show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$." 13194139534816 false] [17592186156982 1100 "For the polynomial $f(x) = -3x^4 + x^2 + 4x,$ show that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" 13194139534816 true] [17592186156982 1145 "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a$, and we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & g(x) &} \\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < & x-a\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq K & x &} \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < \\mid x\\end{vmatrix} < \\delta}\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & &= & -3x^{4} + x^{2} + 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&\\le\\begin{vmatrix}3x^{4} & + & x^{2} & + & 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid x \\mid < 1$, then we have ${\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^2 \\lt & x\\end{vmatrix}}$. Also, since $\\mid x \\mid <1$, we have $\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 < & x\\end{vmatrix}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^4 \\le 3 & x & \\quad\\text{and}\\quad & x & ^2 \\le & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\mid f(x) \\mid \\mid \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & ^{4} + & x & ^{2} + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; \\le 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & + & x & + 4 & x\\end{vmatrix}; = 8 \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}f(x) & \\leq 8 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhenever $\\mid x \\mid < 1$. Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and\n\n$$\n{\\delta = 1}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the definition, and we conclude that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(x)\n$$\n\nas $x\\to 0$." 13194139534816 false] [17592186156982 1145 "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist ${\\textrm{positive}}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta$ such that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|g(x)|}\n\\quad\\textrm{for all}\\quad 0 < |x-a| < \\delta.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{|f(x)| \\leq K|x|}\n \\quad \\textrm{for} \\quad {0 < |x| < \\delta}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|-3{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}+4x| \\\\  & \\le |3{x}^{4}|+|{x}^{2}|+|4x| \\\\  & =3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $|x| < 1,$ then we have ${|x|^2 \\lt |x|}.$ Also, since $|x|<1,$ we have $|x|^4 < |x|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3|x|^4 \\le 3|x|\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n|x|^2 \\le |x|.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le 3|x{|}^{4}+|x{|}^{2}+4|x| \\\\  & \\le 3|x|+|x|+4|x| \\\\  & =8|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)| \\leq 8|x|$ whenever $|x| < 1.$ Thus, taking ${K=8}$ and ${\\delta = 1}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f = \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$" 13194139534816 true] [17592186156983 1100 "Consider the following statement.\n\n> If $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x$, then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$.\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines $XXXXXXXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$ property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line $XXXXX$ and the $XXXXXXX$." 13194139534816 false] [17592186156983 1100 "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x) = e^{x}+ \\sec x,$ then $f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA sketch of a proof of this statement is given below\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\; e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\; \\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(2)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\; f = \\mathcal O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$   \n\nFill in the blanks in the reasoning below that justify each step.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from lines  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/e52e49265a91cd725c6c5ffb89764144b14f9c7ecd39d8f72c4341337c985492.png) and  the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/04029e3a400b01eb4e063bc8609fc8724b1f9c0b0c0291194e330aead3dee346.png) property.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from line ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/69fd9b3718fc120ca9106dcb8c7d74154071ba2454634d3b3a2cc59aa64a522c.png) and the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040950/assets/819c819776622cbb5c29604cb7c5a6ef3bff8554912b649391b8b4f482aa36b0.png)." 13194139534816 true] [17592186156983 1145 "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line  $\\textrm{L3}.$ From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\ne^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L3}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L4}}$ we have\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n\n$$\nf = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0.\n$$" 13194139534816 true] [17592186156983 1145 "Suppose $f(x), g(x), h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation.\n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(k)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f+g = \\mathcal O(h+k)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad fg = \\mathcal O(hk)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The constant multiple property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(cg)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(g)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The reflexive property.\n$f = \\mathcal O(f)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The transitive property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(g) \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n- The absorption property. If\n$f = \\mathcal O(g+h)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad g = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$,\nthen\n$f = \\mathcal O(h)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a$.\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L3}$. From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we have\n$e^{x} = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$,\nand from line $\\textrm{L2}$ we have\n$\\sec x = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\nThus, by lines $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L1 and L3}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the sum property}}$, we obtain\n$f = \\mathcal O(1+1) = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L4}$. From line $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{L4}}$ we have\n$f = \\mathcal O(2)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$.\nSince $2$ is just a constant multiple of $1$, it follows by $\\bbox[4pt,border:solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{the constant multiple property}}$ that\n$f = \\mathcal O(1)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to 0$." 13194139534816 false] [17592186157099 1100 "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n2. $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n3. $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" 13194139534816 false] [17592186157099 1100 "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" 13194139534816 true] [17592186157099 1145 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty$. To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x$. Then, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}$.\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}$, we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1}; = 1 + lim_(x → ∞)(e^{x} + \\ln x); = 1 + ∞; = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((\\ln x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1/x}; = lim_(x → ∞)x(e^{x} + \\ln x + 1); = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{xe^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{x} + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = 0 + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)((\\ln x)^{′})/((e^{x})^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1/x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{xe^{x}}; = 0$.\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534816 false] [17592186157099 1145 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty.$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =1+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x.$  Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1/x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534816 true] [17592186157383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311843 13194139534816 true] [17592186157384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311879 13194139534816 true] [17592186157384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311886 13194139534816 true] [17592186157384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311893 13194139534816 true] [17592186157384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311900 13194139534816 true] [17592186157385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311907 13194139534816 true] [17592186157385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311933 13194139534816 true] [17592186157385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311959 13194139534816 true] [17592186157385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311985 13194139534816 true] [17592186157385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312010 13194139534816 true] [17592186157688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311818 13194139534816 true] [17592186157688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311822 13194139534816 true] [17592186157688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311825 13194139534816 true] [17592186157688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311829 13194139534816 true] [17592186157688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311835 13194139534816 true] [17592186157688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311839 13194139534816 true] [17592186157688 1091 #edb/ref 1759