dca-1423-5cf9-873e-42e35e31e488" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311878 1034 "\\delta =1" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311878 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534816 true] [17592186311878 1111 #uuid "5938ad42-5e9a-5fc5-9773-473f20843394" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311879 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311880 13194139534816 true] [17592186311879 1089 "q-334844" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311879 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534816 true] [17592186311879 1100 "Suppose $f(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0$" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311879 1113 #uuid "4532dbb6-e08c-509a-be1c-9f1169938166" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311879 1145 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is finite, and $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{x}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, while $3x\\to 0.$\nSo\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{4}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311881 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311882 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311883 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311884 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311885 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1041 "selection" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311883 13194139534816 true] [17592186311880 1110 #uuid "104ad863-c700-55ff-8085-06fe59b9d4f9" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311881 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534816 true] [17592186311881 1111 #uuid "0b3805f0-7b98-5525-9db0-9d344a28cccd" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311882 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534816 true] [17592186311882 1111 #uuid "5b963d7f-263b-5cbd-8398-a97be9147a41" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311883 1034 "I only" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311883 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534816 true] [17592186311883 1111 #uuid "a82f3661-9ac3-5dfa-b057-345c37c6608a" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311884 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534816 true] [17592