\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each selected step.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\n2{e}^{4x}=O(2{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand this follows by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{e}^{4x}=O(2{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx=O(2{e}^{4x}).\n$$\n\nSince $f=2{e}^{4x}+x,$ by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=2{e}^{4x}+x=O(2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{4x})=O(4{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nwhich justifies line $\\text{L4}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(4{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand by $\\text{the constant multiple property}$ with $c=4,$ we conclude\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{4x}).\n$$" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311909 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311910 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311911 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311912 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311913 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1041 "field-1" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311913 13194139534816 true] [17592186311908 1110 #uuid "089540ee-6911-5a87-8b20-930cfe6299aa" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311909 1034 "the absorption property" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534816 true] [17592186311909 1111 #uuid "f69450f5-3826-5ec4-9395-3addde505008" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311910 1034 "the constant multiple property" 13194139534816 true] [17592186311910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534816 true] [175921